  <h1>Indoor plant watering</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    Overwatering kills more houseplants than underwatering. The rule that
    matters most is simple: check the soil before you water. Push a finger
    about 5 cm (two inches) into the mix. If it feels damp, wait. If it feels
    dry, water thoroughly until it runs out of the drainage holes — then empty
    the saucer so the plant is not sitting in water.
  </p>

  <h2>The finger test beats a schedule</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    "Water every Sunday" sounds tidy, but plants do not drink on a calendar.
    Light, temperature, pot size, and the season all change how fast soil
    dries. In winter most houseplants need far less water than in summer. A
    weekly check with your finger is more reliable than any fixed schedule.
  </p>

